Do not publish a GPU-LiDAR frame whose readback produced nothing.

The renderer segfaults during flight. 13 of 18 crash reports under
Blocks/Saved/Crashes (2026-08-01 .. 2026-08-07) are the same assertion, on AirSim's
physics thread, never on the game thread:

    Assertion failed: (Index >= 0) & (Index < ArrayNum)
      [File:Runtime/Core/Public/Containers/Array.h] [Line: 1339]
    Array index out of bounds: 42257 into an array of size 0

    FDebug::CheckVerifyFailedImpl2
    ALidarCamera::ProcessCapturedBuffers
    ALidarCamera::UpdateAsync             LidarCamera.cpp:371
    UnrealGPULidarSensor::getPointCloud   UnrealGPULidarSensor.cpp:49
    GPULidarSimple::updateOutput -> World::worldUpdatorAsync

INTO AN ARRAY OF SIZE 0 is the whole diagnosis. Every recorded index (2948, 3405,
8768, 18823, 32088, 42257, 52937, 57062, 58626, 69923, 147705) is a legal offset
into resolution_ x resolution_, so the h_pixel/v_pixel guard is doing its job. The
buffer it guards is simply empty.

It is empty because ServiceAsyncCapture ignores what ReadPixels tells it:

    ENGINE_API virtual bool ReadPixels(TArray<FColor>& OutImageData, ...)
      -- Engine/Source/Runtime/Engine/Public/UnrealClient.h:113

ReadPixels returns false and leaves the destination array EMPTY when the readback
does not complete. Upstream discards that bool and then sets
async_capture_ready_ = true unconditionally, so a failed capture is advertised to
the physics thread as a good one. ProcessCapturedBuffers indexes
async_buffer_2D_depth_[h_pixel + v_pixel * resolution_] and dies. Nothing anywhere
in LidarCamera.cpp ever calls Num() or IsValidIndex on these three buffers -- the
size the reader assumes is only ever inferred from a setting, never checked.

This is not the game thread racing the physics thread over the buffer. A
reallocation mid-loop would report varied nonzero sizes; all eleven distinct
reports say size 0.

Why this stack hits it and many do not: async_capture_mode is not a settings key.
GPULidarSimpleParams.hpp:62 assigns it before the JSON is parsed --

    async_capture_mode = (simmode_name == AirSimSettings::kSimModeTypeMultirotor);

-- so it is hardcoded ON for every multirotor and cannot be turned off from
settings.json. Our sim/ue5/settings.json runs a multirotor with a GPU-LiDAR
(SensorType 8) enabled, which is the configuration this path was written for.
The existing warm-up guard (waited_frames_ >= wait_frames_) is a fixed frame count
standing in for "is the readback working yet", and a fixed count is a guess: this
box shares one 3080 between the renderer, the LiDAR capture and the video witness.

The fix is the check upstream skipped, in two places:

  1. ServiceAsyncCapture -- capture_ok is the AND of every ReadPixels that ran and
     the resulting Num(). async_capture_ready_ takes that value instead of true.
     A frame we could not read is a frame to drop, not a frame to publish.
  2. ProcessCapturedBuffers -- refuse to enter the loop unless each buffer the loop
     will actually index (depth always, intensity and segmentation per their
     generate_ flags, matching the reads at the three index sites) holds at least
     resolution_^2 pixels. Returning false means "no new pointcloud this frame",
     which the caller already handles.

Both are needed. (1) is the bug; (2) makes any future path that gets readiness
wrong cost a dropped scan instead of the process.

Deliberately NOT changed: the FReadSurfaceDataFlags built for the segmentation
readback on the line above it is constructed, configured with SetLinearToGamma(false),
and then never passed to ReadPixels -- so segmentation is read with default flags.
That is a real upstream bug, but fixing it changes segmentation pixel values, which
is a behaviour change and not this crash. Recorded in docs/vendor/cosys-airsim.md.

